Fouling, maldistribution, pressure loss and exchanger service (@th-exchanger-reality: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer, #source "../sources/A-Heat-Transfer-Textbook-Lienhard.lingua#r_7TKQ2D5YRNQ8XVHXNJQDRANWKD", #source "../sources/Guarded-Hot-Plate-and-SRM-1450e-NIST.lingua#r_1PP28H0Y6N7RVY89W9HSQY18E4", #source "../sources/Advanced-Thermodynamics-MIT-2-43.lingua#r_5GXQQ35FEFJM34SWA12X8QHGKC", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "Sensible-latent-and-reactive-thermal-storage.lingua#r_3TJT4KE8M619Y9628FD3N3K3W9") { r_1772HCRCTXFNDXMGCNSDTJBW4W
**Read:** [A Heat Transfer Textbook — Lienhard and Lienhard](../sources/A-Heat-Transfer-Textbook-Lienhard.lingua#r_7TKQ2D5YRNQ8XVHXNJQDRANWKD), complete applicable exchanger methods and limitations; [Guarded-hot-plate evidence — NIST SP 260-201](../sources/Guarded-Hot-Plate-and-SRM-1450e-NIST.lingua#r_1PP28H0Y6N7RVY89W9HSQY18E4), measurement/conditioning reasoning; [Advanced Thermodynamics — MIT 2.43, Spring 2024](../sources/Advanced-Thermodynamics-MIT-2-43.lingua#r_5GXQQ35FEFJM34SWA12X8QHGKC), whole-service performance accounting.

An exchanger's sustained performance depends on more than clean-wall heat-transfer area. Flow maldistribution, bypass, fouling, deposits, corrosion, contact changes and fluid-property variation can alter local and total behavior. Pressure loss can increase pumping power or reduce the actual flow.

Compare clean, degraded and cleaned states at consistent inlet conditions and flow measurements. Infer an overall conductance only with a reconciled energy balance and uncertainty. A performance decline can arise from the process conditions or sensors as well as the exchanger itself.

For an integrated service, include inspection, access, cleaning, compatible materials, replacement and documentation of actual configuration. Thermal performance, leak containment and mechanical integrity are different claims. Full pressure equipment, materials and process design use the shared thermal model and their own complete specialist methods.

